#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd9ec779-6e5b-54f7-8fd9-a5183794b900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400465} {:answer/id #uuid "2f86b6dc-bb9c-5829-8488-ad5ed68bda32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400466} {:answer/id #uuid "ba05598e-f2d4-5884-9220-cb669bdd2471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400467} {:answer/id #uuid "6434ae8b-dd11-5d8d-a8cc-6bab51ffb0aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400468} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f89614-0c4c-5da9-973a-e0bc4c18e8b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd9ec779-6e5b-54f7-8fd9-a5183794b900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400465} :answer-field/id #uuid "c115e153-7737-5c46-8dab-a27a2878d2ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a8a1c12-7f84-5c3b-83f4-8db057221abd" :question/math-academy-id "q-130256" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is **not** linear. To see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2}) & =a[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-ax\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}-bx\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =a[2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2ax\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+2bx\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =a[{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c50bae7c-4e9f-5360-a509-ff44725d8a45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400472} {:answer/id #uuid "180930f4-6ef0-5699-aaf0-df74299a4406" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400473} {:answer/id #uuid "dd59af1e-9a02-50a6-a868-999451ea5ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400474} {:answer/id #uuid "29fac9c5-fa11-5337-b426-2051164249e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400475} {:answer/id #uuid "5975f4ce-bb96-58c1-b253-172e6c5a0d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29fac9c5-fa11-5337-b426-2051164249e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400475} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b85420-0caa-5a47-aa4b-176ffc8735f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d8c3559-a0d3-57b0-8496-a5eb629e4fde" :question/math-academy-id "q-130261" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2xy$\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+xy$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2ax{y}_{1}-2bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x{y}_{1}]+b[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =a[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}]+b[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7281f6-7e60-5d96-9073-2b79663c148b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400479} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1c46db-e09e-574c-8457-d835d0d60cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400480} {:answer/id #uuid "909cb4b3-d84a-5df3-b043-02b797c8f6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400481} {:answer/id #uuid "496f929c-182e-536d-8240-53d72e20d1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400482} {:answer/id #uuid "1e501008-96e1-5873-9523-2270ec047af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e501008-96e1-5873-9523-2270ec047af7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400483} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ddf9b10-51fd-5a37-b528-b67408ad0ada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc4fe478-ecca-5d83-9a4b-47e34a0bc1c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-130448" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below ${a}_{12}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f1ec99-e166-5986-a07d-f07be0dfeb31" :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b941157-8ffb-520c-b2ef-d7a36bdce1a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400486} {:answer/id #uuid "67e651d1-c54c-5fe7-9c72-f5ade7586006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400487} {:answer/id #uuid "37d069ad-58a5-5797-a444-cb0b9739f6ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400488} {:answer/id #uuid "7b89d8ea-f086-5926-b04f-9c29ef947abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400489} {:answer/id #uuid "f467548d-b552-5152-aeb7-b0a1a55536c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b941157-8ffb-520c-b2ef-d7a36bdce1a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400486} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddadcd7e-eab7-5944-aa61-a612ea006ad0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f64f381-0f93-53f2-9bc3-bd26dbdc1549" :question/math-academy-id "q-130462" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -6 & -7 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -18 & -7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\nI. ${R}_{2}:=…$\nII. ${R}_{3}:=…$\nIII. ${R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries below ${a}_{13}=3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 2 & -6 & -7 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -8 & 6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 11 & 10 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 10 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -18 & -7 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2300a1-f31e-5a24-889a-6f45e1e8647b" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Matrix Product as a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cfc179d-5b69-5b59-a4e1-203c8dd4bb22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5y=9 \\\\ x=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400493} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb23989-8b95-58e0-be53-600d7e758325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5y=9 \\\\ x=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400494} {:answer/id #uuid "46e0ac2a-9f8f-5e38-9016-5ba65e5cde4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5y=-9 \\\\ x=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400495} {:answer/id #uuid "8886eb42-23ce-5d40-8b2f-72b0dcb62859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+5y=9 \\\\ x=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400496} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8b1051-a221-5a72-b1f6-4fd5bf2c673e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+5y=9 \\\\ y=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cfc179d-5b69-5b59-a4e1-203c8dd4bb22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5y=9 \\\\ x=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400493} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c4d849c-e24c-5150-9286-220ece393a3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1162f6f-3b06-5edc-926f-fe3ac4ca244f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130528" :question/problem "What is the system of equations represented by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x+5y=9 \\\\ x+0y=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5y=9 \\\\ x=-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "958cdcf3-aaba-5275-85f8-9531f10b3d0e" :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48b1c21b-17a8-5932-8da9-5e616c8cd523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-4{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400500} {:answer/id #uuid "466954de-a984-57a2-be5a-cd8d17a4da88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-4{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400501} {:answer/id #uuid "ca73f35d-ae27-5000-b5a3-91cb21fd303e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400502} {:answer/id #uuid "9d09a102-2216-5c3b-a204-8f6100ed848e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}-4{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400503} {:answer/id #uuid "31db26cf-0604-5eff-9a4a-9453ac889e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31db26cf-0604-5eff-9a4a-9453ac889e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400504} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac253d7f-a67e-5b99-a224-411e8693cccc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0caa2d92-c312-53e1-9069-1f7c037c78ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-130624" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 10 & 3 & 15\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\nI. ${C}_{1}:=…$\nII. ${C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{3}$\nIII. ${C}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${C}_{i}:={C}_{i}+k{C}_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{13}=-2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -1 & 10 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 10 & 3 & 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${C}_{1}:={C}_{1}-2{C}_{3}\\,$ and $\\,{C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69657e5e-ddb2-5905-87d9-718781e63186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400507} {:answer/id #uuid "85f6e2e5-5000-5e4e-823a-50db2fc79262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400508} {:answer/id #uuid "375a7515-6b30-5def-bfb9-8102113b1869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400509} {:answer/id #uuid "4d61a059-dddb-5e13-801e-a014bc95f9c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400510} {:answer/id #uuid "d61776a1-23a9-569a-9478-5925bcddef45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69657e5e-ddb2-5905-87d9-718781e63186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400507} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce429fdf-6b6f-5359-bf73-1a833c32c68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d49dd3c-2d26-5daf-baa6-13e98e158d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-130772" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $P$ and $Q$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $S$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1494e45b-e9c6-535d-9eba-3790fc3b3525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400514} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2aeafd-d65d-5e22-bb2e-c7f378129297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400515} {:answer/id #uuid "15a62809-c816-544c-b95c-2f003c843342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400516} {:answer/id #uuid "97c97b06-f6db-546d-aa97-0f3a35c8b667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400517} {:answer/id #uuid "164d8bbe-3777-525f-8440-8bfe402f43f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$  and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97c97b06-f6db-546d-aa97-0f3a35c8b667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400517} :answer-field/id #uuid "70925ade-385f-5362-ac6e-a022a4d19eae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea3b1565-0857-5d0a-8afe-805754b2cf2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130778" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $A$ and $C$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e0739b-acd3-5213-baab-e978002551c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-12$, $y=6$, and $z=-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400521} {:answer/id #uuid "09aec1aa-dcc4-5694-820b-48b65b045141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=28$, $y=-10$, and $z=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400522} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea67907-7f4c-52b6-b879-e7a1aaa740f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-12$, $y=-10$, and $z=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400523} {:answer/id #uuid "06e6ab1e-4b01-5e52-ba1d-2553cc1a9411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=28$, $y=-6$, and $z=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400524} {:answer/id #uuid "fc215d6c-0ef8-5f56-9172-bb0f8dae360e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=18$, $y=6$, and $z=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09aec1aa-dcc4-5694-820b-48b65b045141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=28$, $y=-10$, and $z=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400522} :answer-field/id #uuid "924e4677-652c-5aa5-ad82-4e9a728fb938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8eff9d12-5ab8-5d85-973e-0e821294f176" :question/math-academy-id "q-130927" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y-z=4 \\\\  & -3x-6y+4z=-8 \\\\  & 3x+7y-z=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z=4 \\\\ y+2z=-2 \\\\ z=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=4$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2(4)=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-10$ and $z=4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(-10)-(4)=4\\,⟹\\,x=28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=28$, $y=-10$, and $z=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "488c9762-554b-5ea6-b414-ce6cbde9bbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e852572-51e3-535c-9be9-f82a128e410a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400528} {:answer/id #uuid "c38d11df-3368-5111-b271-c2942e30b5ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400529} {:answer/id #uuid "221f5136-99d8-51e9-978f-5634a85c5a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400530} {:answer/id #uuid "683d0856-f824-588e-89f5-53ee69683673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400531} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f7a018-3d50-5a97-8f78-2ad6320e0ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the columns are pivot" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "221f5136-99d8-51e9-978f-5634a85c5a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400530} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd67e9a0-4175-5b65-b2b4-59736ca6880c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0565b425-ebb4-5d4a-bad6-acea463e950b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130930" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $A?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $2$ pivots in the $1$st and $3$rd columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "488c9762-554b-5ea6-b414-ce6cbde9bbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e57d00-42e2-52df-952c-42750204443a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400535} {:answer/id #uuid "a3863802-93b4-5373-bcde-0104355b474b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400536} {:answer/id #uuid "9762716d-a441-594e-b7da-17f912c3928a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400537} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d3708e-c3d0-564b-bd88-f92111f6947e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $3$rd, $4$th, and $5$th" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400538} {:answer/id #uuid "5e65e301-be51-5d5c-a153-d8c519c07444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the columns are pivot" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3863802-93b4-5373-bcde-0104355b474b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400536} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba0fda7f-cbbe-50a6-a823-a14a0b2a64d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ae52a94-ae23-5f16-93c9-2dbde81b5ce4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130935" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $M$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $M?$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 & 8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $M$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 & 8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "488c9762-554b-5ea6-b414-ce6cbde9bbd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47622800-9004-565a-a9eb-b240bc35fae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, and $5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400542} {:answer/id #uuid "66cf73aa-6696-58c4-b6d1-5a62d04ecffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400543} {:answer/id #uuid "bc827db8-4204-5f1c-9e33-bce5c39c4b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, $4$th, and $5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400544} {:answer/id #uuid "784c7c4b-377b-5289-ab56-9dd00ea13562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the columns are pivot" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400545} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6e056d-d971-54e4-a9ac-b9c029160238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc827db8-4204-5f1c-9e33-bce5c39c4b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd, $3$rd, $4$th, and $5$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400544} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd988c2-8541-5d9c-a62d-e7a2548b81e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe1c5404-bbff-566c-b120-dde7595ffb3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130946" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are the pivot columns of $A?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -9 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $4$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, $4$th, and $5$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -9 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Non-Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e027a524-8226-530f-8bc1-156c5fd62fd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400549} {:answer/id #uuid "abec9e6f-585d-579d-ba67-a7171482e670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400550} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ae8fb3-1706-5ab8-bf51-347d556cc188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400551} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9f17c6-99c5-5c23-9ab2-fc4a4ba0097d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400552} {:answer/id #uuid "80bf3cc2-bd4a-58d4-8821-bedc396a54f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f9f17c6-99c5-5c23-9ab2-fc4a4ba0097d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400552} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d5343c6-514b-5142-b39c-cac725e3880e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02830b84-264a-5296-8703-bbcf95ce7d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-130954" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $P$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $P$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $1$st column."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5f0d2e9-1cf6-5fe4-85cc-f401382f39b2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Non-Pivot Columns of a Matrix in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9f7d878-00f4-542b-8edf-3af17fe0109d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400556} {:answer/id #uuid "72d87fbc-4b88-5bc5-9012-5cf5dcb8fa96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400557} {:answer/id #uuid "07fc175c-99d2-576d-bf97-0bf7ed21babe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400558} {:answer/id #uuid "8987d718-6753-5003-aba1-920a4bda3fd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400559} {:answer/id #uuid "99bc6b73-3d0e-57d4-97fb-808b0c314e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9f7d878-00f4-542b-8edf-3af17fe0109d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400556} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f56b5f1-cd7e-5c89-a46d-d954a6720fb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cff88abe-cb9f-5f2b-aa6a-898f213ad5e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-130955" :question/problem "The row echelon form matrix $A$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $A$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $2$nd and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $3$rd column."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e377eccc-3189-56b6-b479-71605910738f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400563} {:answer/id #uuid "74fefd89-78b3-5c97-883c-68b88daa0fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400564} {:answer/id #uuid "a20da691-65d2-548d-9a18-553c5730b381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400565} {:answer/id #uuid "8db29ded-36e3-5353-b386-e022a9b25e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400566} {:answer/id #uuid "c99e3be9-96bf-5cd2-aea1-7adeba26d642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74fefd89-78b3-5c97-883c-68b88daa0fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400564} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ba78ad2-0850-53db-9d8e-7e73850a3220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30c6fcc7-4b73-56ac-a7b7-837e8d6bcc9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130998" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\nI. $5{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1$\nII. ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=0,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0$\nIII. $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=1$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9f6d29f-2121-5953-b29d-e5fe2ae8cff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{x}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400570} {:answer/id #uuid "fb108ca1-064a-5a1f-b4a8-c9d68416f398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2+x\\sqrt{x}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400571} {:answer/id #uuid "99a560b9-f442-53ea-9413-22536b9867f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+3\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400572} {:answer/id #uuid "d3331cb4-e07f-5e65-9fab-eabb5092ba87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{3{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400573} {:answer/id #uuid "75ebe5a8-11df-5c7c-8635-490c08eb5669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x+2}{\\sqrt{x}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb108ca1-064a-5a1f-b4a8-c9d68416f398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2+x\\sqrt{x}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400571} :answer-field/id #uuid "33649e08-48a2-55ba-ae41-136334d74883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29af1809-de4f-574a-839e-a36c55c6c73a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131030" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ are solutions to the equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0$ for $x>0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}\\frac{1}{x}+{c}_{2}\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+\\sqrt{x}=\\frac{2+x\\sqrt{x}}{x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "669a6565-67da-5b08-868f-363dad684dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400577} {:answer/id #uuid "d22fb0cb-3d27-521e-88b3-6b3d9e757baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400578} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5c17f3-3439-59bd-96c0-52ac25224fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400579} {:answer/id #uuid "3bc3447b-2d67-5fcc-9f49-6c6341b7219a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400580} {:answer/id #uuid "75a25c3a-a40e-586e-8e37-d0e3a0077526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75a25c3a-a40e-586e-8e37-d0e3a0077526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400581} :answer-field/id #uuid "793064b4-d3a3-564d-97d7-62c9c6cdc59c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0ea85a-8646-5af0-8c5d-b95cfa962333" :question/math-academy-id "q-131032" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{3}=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efb825ec-4af1-502e-84e4-c2363e24558e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400584} {:answer/id #uuid "a0005f3d-2f6a-5da0-ad19-56ff2d7b1c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400585} {:answer/id #uuid "40bc473f-1c61-5b45-af07-0768b0375c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400586} {:answer/id #uuid "2ada282e-9a0b-53a0-acf3-911a8863351b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400587} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb7851c-f9c7-53a1-b68c-803e4833549a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0005f3d-2f6a-5da0-ad19-56ff2d7b1c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400585} :answer-field/id #uuid "40b63710-ca14-5f20-99f5-64eef01983af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19a51c3a-961d-51e8-839d-dcbc2e643a8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-131188" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-72x.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-72x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x-72 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d89fcce3-b832-5e4c-bf7c-02c1c17153f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400591} {:answer/id #uuid "fd55f488-68a4-5b1e-9bbb-f31e747d2214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400592} {:answer/id #uuid "1facde6d-5525-541b-93d6-30090cb94ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400593} {:answer/id #uuid "fa042a55-7109-5f70-97f2-337042cc265e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400594} {:answer/id #uuid "be65614c-b0a6-53df-8fb4-0615b69a4d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd55f488-68a4-5b1e-9bbb-f31e747d2214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400592} :answer-field/id #uuid "80b83c99-19f6-547f-98fa-adc91ffefc5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42598c74-a5de-588f-a397-c6d324d9491a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131273" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)=({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4})}^{′}{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){({e}^{4{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+(8x)({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =[2x+8{x}^{3}-10x]{e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 8x(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,-1,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35bc5be3-2fe5-58fc-b85a-64fce1c7784d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400598} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee4be77-eda8-5477-a3be-58be37102e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400599} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4d6b54-8a7a-519d-b637-e37bde3dbb2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400600} {:answer/id #uuid "df41476c-0271-5fb9-ba37-452038f9f479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400601} {:answer/id #uuid "643510db-e574-5619-acbb-62a64f7e0730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "643510db-e574-5619-acbb-62a64f7e0730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400602} :answer-field/id #uuid "e34b1c74-ee10-528c-9c1b-188a96803527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "190c1f09-7ea5-53d8-a079-57d65ed02079" :question/math-academy-id "q-131302" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $P,$ given by\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $P$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $P$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d865bfb4-6ff5-577a-a992-c481af8842b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Singular 3x3 System of Equations with Two Pivots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee44784e-c13a-5b12-af97-8b32c8c0a5be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7z+3 \\\\ 2z-2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400605} {:answer/id #uuid "230fd32d-be4a-579c-925a-53e385a20db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7z-3 \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400606} {:answer/id #uuid "c1affba9-f056-51a7-a549-36a67d2e8dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7z-3 \\\\ 2-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400607} {:answer/id #uuid "c81a8969-331d-5a2b-a2ad-56d7d447802b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7z-3 \\\\ 2-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400608} {:answer/id #uuid "74fab224-971f-5289-87ed-552b598c900e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3-7z \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "230fd32d-be4a-579c-925a-53e385a20db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7z-3 \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400606} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb79457-f181-5b77-98a7-2247b1af411b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a5eae7d8-0877-5f8b-8a7a-fca7737eb497" :question/math-academy-id "q-131485" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form. The system corresponding to $M$ is \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+7z=-3 \\\\ 0x+y-2z=2 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+7z=-3 \\\\ y-2z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-2z=2\\,⇒\\,y=2z+2\n$$\n\nand from the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx+7z=-3\\,⇒\\,x=-7z-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-7z-3 \\\\ y=2z+2 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7z-3 \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f742e7-89c6-543b-b43a-f41c27f35abf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Singular 3x3 System of Equations with One Pivot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e094e93-8ced-5de7-8bbd-0b45c329cfff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400612} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd2f927-f231-5b80-9010-2d3a5ee3c4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400613} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0f3ab0-4578-57b2-96d9-136b48cbe0b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400614} {:answer/id #uuid "177d9d96-5f02-53c6-b377-f4b0a23620b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400615} {:answer/id #uuid "713dc755-61ac-526d-bf07-e1bd0d805dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffd2f927-f231-5b80-9010-2d3a5ee3c4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400613} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bf381b7-5f27-5de3-b285-a0155244b022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a36d5833-a8f6-5e41-9f0c-9e264c1ac49c" :question/math-academy-id "q-131499" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-8y+6z=-4 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x-8y+6z=-4\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=4y-3z-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4y-3z-2 \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$, $z$ (free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4y-3z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f742e7-89c6-543b-b43a-f41c27f35abf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Singular 3x3 System of Equations with One Pivot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48980080-3b29-5d93-8895-6dc8d852d470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-3y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400619} {:answer/id #uuid "e332c64f-7ba6-5dbb-85f7-2fda0b6da246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400620} {:answer/id #uuid "3abac20c-2ea5-5660-86e2-4798e8e9514d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-3y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400621} {:answer/id #uuid "66c6dc1b-675e-55c7-974d-d4c469830584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y-2z-3 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400622} {:answer/id #uuid "d146ccb7-a362-5b17-993b-13a2c5e5f08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d146ccb7-a362-5b17-993b-13a2c5e5f08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400623} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e15572-4a4b-502d-8485-53598d56e2c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd76a088-2631-55dd-b133-6b775b2a304e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131502" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y+2z=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove these equations from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\nx-3y+2z=-1\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables $y$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y,z.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\nx=3y-2z-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3y-2z-1 \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y,z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa91091b-5368-5d80-b87d-08b3524177f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With One Pivot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bae6d20c-cc44-51fa-982c-3eec6a0de3e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2-2y+3z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400626} {:answer/id #uuid "b919b598-418f-5d14-b89f-506f59a55363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2y-3z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400627} {:answer/id #uuid "73bc07cb-54e5-5399-9562-2cbafc5a3fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400628} {:answer/id #uuid "496ac6ec-2478-58cd-800e-05e7f0180390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400629} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbb251f-ba33-5470-9ec0-a9bafb5f9ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2y+3z+2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b919b598-418f-5d14-b89f-506f59a55363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2y-3z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400627} :answer-field/id #uuid "452119fd-5ef0-59d8-810c-92339d03e4d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b85625c7-6844-5453-a702-eb2fc4d7eb1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131650" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y+12z=-8 \\\\ x-2y+3z=-2 \\\\ -2x+4y-6z=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 12 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 12 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 12 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3z=-2 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2y-3z-2 \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y-3z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d174f1e-8230-59a5-b790-dfbaab4e9521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400633} {:answer/id #uuid "244aab6d-90be-578a-8092-166a36c8c9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400634} {:answer/id #uuid "83dd970a-9032-5161-9210-85e901723065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4y+3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400635} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7e3b2b-d1a1-5446-8ee4-32636f7b1b2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400636} {:answer/id #uuid "0e38c584-fc56-5df5-a079-f3072bdddfc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "244aab6d-90be-578a-8092-166a36c8c9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400634} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a4567f4-6572-5d9e-8018-4068c6812f07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0feb9ee-8e97-5292-aff6-05655689f05b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131712" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+8y=-6 \\\\  & -4x-16y=12 \\\\  & 6x+24y=-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4y-3 \\\\ y=y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "461eef6c-aefb-5663-8d03-db54f697d105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400640} {:answer/id #uuid "b3d3c662-b67b-5b81-a307-d2b8efde6388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400641} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcd7fd9-5df8-5756-a9f3-48cc31c9e67e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400642} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9dcc22-2e73-56c0-825d-9e630c431eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400643} {:answer/id #uuid "56e99c2d-29cc-5ded-a5f5-80ae572f982d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbcd7fd9-5df8-5756-a9f3-48cc31c9e67e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400642} :answer-field/id #uuid "97478289-921c-5024-949c-f21308c68969" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd791ebf-f827-5528-ae25-d3ac1f74c5cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-131720" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-6y-2z=4 \\\\  & 3x-9y-3z=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The system is consistent independent\nII. The system has no solution\nIII. The system is consistent dependent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -9 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have two free variables, $y$ and $z.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baed0cbf-38ec-5d81-9809-43dafa9379a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400647} {:answer/id #uuid "586150d0-53a3-501e-a821-68c180a394cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400648} {:answer/id #uuid "a39c1a3e-21c9-5508-8921-b8a05957b672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400649} {:answer/id #uuid "2247b748-8cef-53eb-82f9-2487685f5057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400650} {:answer/id #uuid "77d955a7-8c55-5d69-9764-f442198cee4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586150d0-53a3-501e-a821-68c180a394cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400648} :answer-field/id #uuid "62ec4d2b-adce-53b9-bdc4-0b08d9bbcf9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9b47374-5548-52b6-9b51-17d923fcd37b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131721" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y=-1 \\\\  & 3x+7y=1 \\\\  & -2x-4y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The system is consistent independent\nII. The system has no solution\nIII. The system is consistent dependent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ y=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=4$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=-9.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c927d7e-5c4b-5882-8721-adc2b5fd92e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400654} {:answer/id #uuid "64d69900-4157-5d22-aa0e-f4d914c8f721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400655} {:answer/id #uuid "2aba7c87-fdf8-526c-bf60-804628ebce56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400656} {:answer/id #uuid "fea224bb-b24d-5ae7-9e7c-a1698eef7494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400657} {:answer/id #uuid "a62e5405-e20d-5e9c-833b-ebb37c50b5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a62e5405-e20d-5e9c-833b-ebb37c50b5ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400658} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb13c3f2-95da-5f1e-8dd8-8f023abfea3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4890e1ff-ed9a-5558-acd1-bcef01439e04" :question/math-academy-id "q-131722" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x-3y=6 \\\\  & -2x+y=-3 \\\\  & 4x-4y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The system is consistent dependent\nII. The system is consistent independent\nIII. The system has no solution" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=-1$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=1.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc2c4fc2-a754-53f6-ab25-c364e85fb49f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400661} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3b49b7-2177-58c7-9dfd-a4e06235e607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400662} {:answer/id #uuid "046dbcd6-e3df-5486-9381-db7ac7f22442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400663} {:answer/id #uuid "946cdf15-ce31-5c87-88df-3ce8a6a5154d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨0,-1⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400664} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebc0bb1-5876-5366-9608-8b5ebf54b913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,-1⟩$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc2c4fc2-a754-53f6-ab25-c364e85fb49f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400661} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e8d157-633b-58f4-9968-e3a163c79d0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e19d212-99bc-5ec6-ba7f-76b0f4f4e8f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132204" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,0)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩,$ since the point $P(0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,0⟩+1\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66512e2-661f-5bb9-a7ee-3f76eb96d1ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400668} {:answer/id #uuid "a43f9535-88b8-5223-a7ce-2e008288589b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400669} {:answer/id #uuid "c73a8a16-83c6-597c-bfd3-b995e0a8d790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400670} {:answer/id #uuid "da64ed04-930d-5f69-8aa5-1534bad7fdb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400671} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0f1ee6-4f9d-5784-96cb-23288a1cac60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c73a8a16-83c6-597c-bfd3-b995e0a8d790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400670} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d6e03b-d7a1-55cc-9b86-b95900f2d614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e994daf9-6dc5-55a7-aade-020d8278b5a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132666" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)={e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}$ is strictly decreasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)={e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}(4x-6),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}(4x-6)<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exponential function is always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n4x-6<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-6 & <0 \\\\ 4x & <6 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d7c758-6513-5b62-9838-0f0e3e990c96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400675} {:answer/id #uuid "79246e8a-df03-50c3-a1b1-e08f5dedc89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\sqrt{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400676} {:answer/id #uuid "4af42b3a-0a5c-585b-b625-a30cb6ff9720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400677} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc9b135-ebc1-5040-b029-e3841fd75c3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400678} {:answer/id #uuid "65da45d5-5844-595b-8bdf-71df5cfdf77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65da45d5-5844-595b-8bdf-71df5cfdf77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400679} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f397fc-f0b4-510a-8cdc-2eadab74ebfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ca3eeff-b7f5-5d53-aae2-987010ec68f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132667" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$ is strictly decreasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 4x \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{2}+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{2}+1}<0.\n$$\n\nThe function $2{x}^{2}+1$ is always positive. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\n4x<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6938ada6-d242-5a29-b3cc-1005ff1beb10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.69$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400682} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c15084-f362-505f-8950-6df3b2548ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.74$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400683} {:answer/id #uuid "e01f491c-202f-5132-a3f5-3f0f2930aa15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.65$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400684} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea6bf2-0abb-5dd3-8132-52b2488943a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.57$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400685} {:answer/id #uuid "7b383bc1-6fb9-59e8-b057-fb6dccdf74a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.79$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6938ada6-d242-5a29-b3cc-1005ff1beb10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.69$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400682} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb566678-fdf6-552d-aad6-4bf9b9328ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42e998b4-da64-533d-80e4-6303495d71cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132852" :question/problem "The lifetime of a lithium-ion battery produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ years and a standard deviation of $0.5$ years.\nAdditionally, $3%$ of batteries last less than $1.75$ years. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a randomly selected battery. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<1.75)=0.03.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.75) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)=-1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & =-1.8808 \\\\ 1.75-\\mu  & =-0.9404 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈2.69.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6028dff8-a045-5c36-b03a-7cc1e9d875d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.27$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400689} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfc67ac-b865-541b-abd3-49ba35181b7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3.73$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400690} {:answer/id #uuid "7922a355-3dcf-5ee9-a956-87ba8d17b65c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4.85$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400691} {:answer/id #uuid "be6f8eda-cb13-5113-8a9a-1637beaf9d1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4.41$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400692} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddb9a99-6c2a-5cfd-b39f-b1b603fa424c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.38$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6028dff8-a045-5c36-b03a-7cc1e9d875d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$5.27$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400689} :answer-field/id #uuid "27f6174e-7ddd-5db0-b8a0-9648c670a8d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50c01113-7eba-59c9-b302-374966a56f93" :question/math-academy-id "q-132856" :question/problem "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.7.$ In addition, in $94%$ of cases the admission fee is between $$0.75\\mu $ and $$1.25\\mu .$ Find the average cinema admission fee, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent an admission fee. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu )=0.94.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.75\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.94 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.47 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.5+0.47 \\\\ P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.25\\mu ) & =0.97 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.97 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.97 \\\\ \\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.97)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.97)≈1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.25\\mu }{0.7} & =1.8808 \\\\ 0.25\\mu  & =1.31656 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.31656}{0.25} \\\\  & ≈5.27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Constants in First-Order Partial Derivative Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2c399e-121a-557b-920f-152b81063128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=5,\\,b=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400696} {:answer/id #uuid "304c3881-343e-5f37-8917-97e64c6106bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=3,\\,b=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400697} {:answer/id #uuid "28631350-323a-5b02-b5b9-57ea02a9e138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400698} {:answer/id #uuid "04ecd13a-ecde-56eb-a1ff-be81f2b3b9a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=6,\\,b=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400699} {:answer/id #uuid "004f0530-cdcb-544a-bbbf-8ccea866aba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28631350-323a-5b02-b5b9-57ea02a9e138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=2,\\,b=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400698} :answer-field/id #uuid "3228e72d-592f-5149-97de-0d06f9bc95c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7b780a7-788b-5d60-b089-16f56470436d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133221" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n{f}_{x}(x,y)=axy-5{y}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}-bxy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(axy-5{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =ax-10y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-bxy) \\\\  & =2x-by\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax-10y=2x-by.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ respectively, we see that $a=2$ and $b=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28adc95e-e6e5-580c-9eed-55a3d12d5232" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trend Line Equations to Make a Prediction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4c4d81f-1807-5985-bc75-f78a53aceac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400703} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8e005e-3461-54dc-ade3-c36922d765ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400704} {:answer/id #uuid "f3927082-1031-515d-8b67-7ab60167c394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$11$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400705} {:answer/id #uuid "72fb5990-5df5-5b7d-af99-8c775eef439e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400706} {:answer/id #uuid "baae8426-6809-58f7-b30c-71ec0d63e5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baae8426-6809-58f7-b30c-71ec0d63e5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ decades" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400707} :answer-field/id #uuid "276c6bfa-9bed-5b15-b91d-56f8ec1aea0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87bad21a-36f4-5e06-9484-4b7826190ce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-133929" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce3aec85d418c8f51988f819c04403f3f3ea2e0c0e35165ed729fc1e78d0f12f.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of an artwork $y,$ in thousands of dollars, and the number of decades $x$ since it was first created. The equation of the trend line is $y=3x+5.$ \n\nAssuming this trend continues, estimate the time it takes for the artwork to be worth $$32\\,000.$" :question/worked-solution "To predict the time that must pass before the artwork is worth $$32\\,000,$ we need to substitute $y=32$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x+5 \\\\ 32 & =3x+5 \\\\ 3x & =27 \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes approximately $9$ decades for the artwork to have a value of $$32\\,000.$ \n\nNote: The point where $y=32$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cc70bda-40cf-5860-9a47-25f290c71041" :knowledge-point/title "Describing an Entry of a Matrix in Index Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53bc7c4a-ec03-5241-b616-ab551e2b27cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400710} {:answer/id #uuid "3b70af93-f0df-596a-b13f-d4cefebb16dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400711} {:answer/id #uuid "710c1a23-9554-5a6a-91ac-2f16e51847e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400712} {:answer/id #uuid "64372579-911a-5351-8eec-53ed6b8d3b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400713} {:answer/id #uuid "437a5fab-d07b-5f08-b95c-d799c5a714f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "437a5fab-d07b-5f08-b95c-d799c5a714f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400714} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c4d5fd-03ba-51d6-8190-92d4e4a4e499" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76f61515-4e83-5716-8dff-d44543d61f70" :question/math-academy-id "q-13499" :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $A$ given below. Describe it in terms of ${a}_{ij}.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 14 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 17 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The entry lies at the intersection of the $3$rd row and $4$th column. Hence, we have ${a}_{34}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d441155e-dbcb-598d-afc7-949ee4eddbee" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c8857b7-5f5d-5457-8dd6-2b4309d575d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400717} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f420ae-7985-53ba-8421-2ceb6b4b4f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400718} {:answer/id #uuid "57bed31e-8ef7-54da-8f39-982e238c1dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400719} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdee401-1c5c-5788-8574-ed2dd56862ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400720} {:answer/id #uuid "75082212-5bbc-5f18-98d7-7d1fb55b442b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57bed31e-8ef7-54da-8f39-982e238c1dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400719} :answer-field/id #uuid "106db307-f502-5d2c-ad7c-d17a4f3844ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "183a141f-bd68-52b7-ae90-0aa68787bc11" :question/math-academy-id "q-135228" :question/problem "A marble is projected vertically downward from a height of $245\\,\\text{m}$ with an initial velocity of $24.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How long will it take for the marble to hit the ground? \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the marble is $u=24.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The marble covers the distance of $s=245\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 245 & =(24.5)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 245 & =24.5t+4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ 50(4.9) & =5(4.9)t+4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ 50\\cancel{(4.9)} & =5\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ 50 & =5t+{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+5t-50 & =0 \\\\ (t+10)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-10,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the marble hits the ground after $t=5$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe060a57-c5fa-5e13-816e-242c1ae0f810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400724} {:answer/id #uuid "69365ed6-d82e-5f46-8caf-0e16ac9d1e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5∓4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400725} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c8a5e0-33df-5e97-ae29-be024ad7b1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400726} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed7cbfd-bc73-59a1-9f19-e8c4e4e1fb8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1∓4\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400727} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4204d9-9e4e-5bbe-b5eb-e89b156fba7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7c8a5e0-33df-5e97-ae29-be024ad7b1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400726} :answer-field/id #uuid "717a76be-6f3b-501d-a9ed-63a7517d5688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db2db2cc-8132-5f94-95c8-6b73da358424" :question/math-academy-id "q-135440" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f74dc1-50df-595e-8161-b8f89331daa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-5\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400731} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7a423c-75c4-5e5a-b59c-84187ba85ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}5\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 2∓3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400732} {:answer/id #uuid "42d7d073-ef3b-52cf-9bc8-76d763c638be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}5\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400733} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ebde54-8171-5261-b9aa-5161a997d41a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}5\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ ∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400734} {:answer/id #uuid "de8841